{"name":"Roman Snail","hex":"#a49593","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"roman","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Upright, as opposed to italic.","Of or related to the Latin alphabet or roman numerals."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["One of the main three types used for the Latin alphabet (the others being italics and blackletter), in which the ascenders are mostly straight.","Ellipsis of roman numeral."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/roman"},{"term":"snail","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of very many animals (either hermaphroditic or nonhermaphroditic), of the class Gastropoda, having a coiled shell.","A slow person; a sluggard."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move or travel very slowly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/snail"}],"entries":[{"title":"Helix pomatia","blocks":[{"text":"Helix pomatia, known as the Roman snail, Burgundy snail, or escargot, is a species of large, air-breathing stylommatophoran land snail belonging to the family Helicidae and native to Europe. It is characterized by a globular brown shell."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/db/Helix_pomatia_89a.jpg/330px-Helix_pomatia_89a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Helix_pomatia"},{"title":"Snail","blocks":[{"text":"A snail is a shelled gastropod. The name is most often applied to land snails, terrestrial pulmonate gastropod molluscs. However, the common name snail is also used for most of the members of the molluscan class Gastropoda that have a coiled shell that is large enough for the animal to retract completely into. When the word \"snail\" is used in this most general sense, it includes not just land snails but also numerous species of sea snails and freshwater snails. Gastropods that naturally lack a shell, or have only an internal shell, are mostly called slugs, and land snails that have only a very small shell are sometimes called semi-slugs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Snail.jpg/330px-Snail.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Snail"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Helix-pomatia_Presence_in_European_countries.png/500px-Helix-pomatia_Presence_in_European_countries.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/Helix-pomatia_Presence_in_European_countries.png/500px-Helix-pomatia_Presence_in_European_countries.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Distribution map of H. pomatia showing the European countries where the species is present","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHelix-pomatia_Presence_in_European_countries.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/SnailMeeting.jpg/500px-SnailMeeting.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/SnailMeeting.jpg/1280px-SnailMeeting.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/SnailMeeting.jpg/500px-SnailMeeting.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"In Odenwald, Germany","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASnailMeeting.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Helix_pomatia_eye_microscopical.jpg/500px-Helix_pomatia_eye_microscopical.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Helix_pomatia_eye_microscopical.jpg/1280px-Helix_pomatia_eye_microscopical.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=th